  "worked_solution": "First, let's simplify the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36{x}^{2} & >49 \\\\ {x}^{2} & >\\frac{49}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use either of two methods to solve the inequality.\n\n*Method 1 - Solving an Absolute Value Equation*\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{36}} \\\\ |x| & >\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have \n\n$$\nx<-\\frac{7}{6}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},+\\infty ).$\n\n*Method 2 - Picturing a Quadratic Curve*\n\nStarting from ${x}^{2}>\\frac{49}{36}$ and moving all terms to the left-hand side, we reach\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}>0.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $\\,y={x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}\\,$ and find its roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{6})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-\\frac{7}{6})(x+\\frac{7}{6}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots are  $x=\\pm \\frac{7}{6}.$ \n\nSince the leading coefficient of ${x}^{2}-\\frac{49}{36}$   is positive, the parabola opens upward. Hence, the solution set to the given inequality ($y>0$) lies outside the roots and does not include them:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13965374/assets/14702e7d185d38587cf10642c8a455d9d25a51d2a8eebbf7e0e54a79223ea56e.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{6})\\cup (\\frac{7}{6},\\infty ).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
